WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.692 --> 00:00:08.150
STEPHANIE WONG: So
I think maybe we

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:11.030
can start by giving the audience
some context about Quantum

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:11.637
Metric.

00:00:11.637 --> 00:00:13.220
Can you tell us a
little bit about how

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:15.505
it got started and
your push to the cloud?

00:00:15.505 --> 00:00:16.880
MARIO CIABARRA:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.850
So we started in 2015.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:20.990
And really, the idea--

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:26.010
I started my career in retail,
in the early '90s, I think.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.470
It's been a while.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:29.210
And there's this
magic that happens

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:31.010
when you walk into
a store and you

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:33.840
are seeking to purchase
some specific good, clothing

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.340
or jewelry.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:39.170
I started my career out in
the retail clothing industry.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:41.662
You'd be buying an airline
ticket, engaging with a bank,

00:00:41.662 --> 00:00:43.370
and there's this
special touch that we've

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.860
perfected over
millennia that, when

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.680
I had that physical
engagement with the person,

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.470
I can understand when
they have a need, when

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:56.070
they are looking for help from
an associate, or et cetera.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.390
And as we have progressed
on to digital, how do we

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.640
deliver these incredible
unique experiences,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.379
these touches that we have
in our physical engagement?

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.360
How do we do that well online?

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.840
And so that was the challenge
that we set out to do.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.000
And you know, there's
a picture here

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.520
of what it looked like in 2015.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.770
And maybe some of you that are
in startups can relate to this.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.400
This is, you know, two guys
in a room with a Kanban board,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.950
just chugging away
at code and design.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.240
So it was a really fun
start back in 2015.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.260
We've gone a long
way since then.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.438
And so we've had some
incredible success.

00:01:33.438 --> 00:01:35.730
So, just to kind of catch up
and just in the last year,

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.240
we've had 400% growth
year over year.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.460
We've been fortunate enough to
take $26 million of funding.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.490
And we have 80-plus
experts around the world

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.680
to help our customers
engage in delivering

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.450
their best on digital.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:52.575
STEPHANIE WONG: And that
was your series A funding?

00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:53.790
That seems like a big number.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:54.370
MARIO CIABARRA: It was.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:54.980
STEPHANIE WONG: Can you
tell us about that a bit?

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:55.855
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah.

00:01:55.855 --> 00:01:59.030
You know, we've had so much
success at enterprises,

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.700
engaging with some
of the best brands--

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.330
United Airlines, Alaska,
eBay, Western Union, StubHub,

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.790
Ticketmaster, Bed, Bath.

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.130
We've had such an
incredible engagement

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:14.090
of how we empower those large
enterprises at delivering

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.190
their best on digital.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:22.520
And when you get that kind
of success with the largest

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.700
of brands-- and even
being a small company--

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.450
there's some excitement.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.250
And so with that, we
were fortunate enough

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.500
to have-- one of the best
venture firms out there

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:34.450
of private equity is
Insight Venture Partners.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.263
So, really blessed to have so
much success at an early stage.

00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:38.180
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.490
And is that your team
in a cube formation?

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:42.230
How long did it
take you to do that?

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.438
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so we
like to have a lot of fun

00:02:44.438 --> 00:02:45.070
at Quantum.

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:48.200
And so this one is just one
of our team get-togethers.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.150
And we all-- we
threw up a drone.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.620
And it was quite fun.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.180
So I think one of the great
things about startups--

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:56.930
and you know, really,
anyone's workplace--

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:58.180
it's got to have a lot of fun.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.460
So we do that here at Quantum.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:02:59.588
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:02:59.588 --> 00:03:01.725
So I see you had two
people on that 2015 photo.

00:03:01.725 --> 00:03:02.600
And now you've grown.

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.790
And talk about your
push to the cloud

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.515
and where you started
compared to now.

00:03:06.515 --> 00:03:09.830
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so I
think we all have our strengths

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:11.180
and weaknesses.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.330
I know my weakness is
not-- it's not about,

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.710
I don't design infrastructure.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.960
I don't design data centers.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.910
And so I'm embarrassed
to show this picture.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.070
But literally, this
is just last week our,

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:27.860
quote, "data center" on prem,
in our Colorado Springs office.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:29.720
And the funny story
about it recently

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.730
is that there was
a bombogenesis.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.240
There was a big storm
in Colorado Springs.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:37.550
And we literally
had to get someone

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.710
to drive a few miles
in a crazy storm

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:42.128
to hit a power switch
somewhere in this closet

00:03:42.128 --> 00:03:43.170
for our development tool.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.960
So as we all worked from home,
we got the support we needed.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.380
So this is how I know
that we weren't meant

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:49.840
to have an on-prem solution.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.370
But there are some
other reasons, too.

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:56.990
We have-- before I jump into
some of the other reasons,

00:03:56.990 --> 00:04:00.140
I guess our journey really
began with the same journey

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.570
that most of the folks that
have onboarded themselves

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.580
onto Google Cloud
have engaged with.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:08.000
And it was really about, we knew
as a data analytics company,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.460
we were going to have massive
amounts of scale coming in.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:14.690
And how would we be able
to elastically scale?

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.910
We didn't have, at the
time, tens of millions

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.720
of dollars of funding to go
build a crazy data center

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.580
to handle that level of scale.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.570
And at the end of
the day, how do we

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:28.850
get folks that are in
the financial services

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.330
industries, that are
massive retailers-- how

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.120
do we get them comfortable with
deploying onto our platform

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:38.770
when we talk about security
and privacy and GDPR

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.152
and really great challenges
that we face in technology?

00:04:41.152 --> 00:04:42.860
How do we get them
comfortable with that?

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.870
And knowing that
Google literally

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.055
has laser beam-protected
floors-- like,

00:04:47.055 --> 00:04:48.430
we don't have them
in our office.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:50.055
I don't know if you
have them in yours.

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:53.870
But also even down
to custom-built,

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.862
embedded hardware to make sure
that the hardware hasn't been

00:04:56.862 --> 00:04:58.570
tampered with, that
it's not been hacked,

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:01.730
et cetera, which if you've been
seeing the news, it happens.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:03.853
And so how do we get
our enterprise customers

00:05:03.853 --> 00:05:05.520
comfortable with that
level of security?

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.562
So that was kind of the
beginning of our journey.

00:05:07.562 --> 00:05:10.590
And I'm excited to share a
little more today about where

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:11.970
we kind of fell into.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:15.240
There's much more to cloud
than just, I would almost say,

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.767
these menial things
of hey, look.

00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:17.850
We can have reduced costs.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:18.900
We can scale quickly.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.748
These are, maybe, table stakes.

00:05:20.748 --> 00:05:22.290
But there are some
really cool things

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.420
that help us think different
about the challenges

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.920
that we face and that we solve
for our enterprise customers.

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:27.580
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.160
And just for the
audience, is anyone

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.830
familiar with seeing that closet
photo in your own offices?

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.300
I'm sure it's pretty common.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:34.992
And we're also
familiar with that

00:05:34.992 --> 00:05:37.200
need to scale very quickly
in a short amount of time.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.905
Can you elaborate
more on this amount

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:41.280
of scale of traffic and data
that you've seen at Quantum?

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:42.947
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah,
I'm super excited.

00:05:42.947 --> 00:05:43.560
I love scale.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:46.500
I'm passionate about taking
on large amounts of data.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.380
And how do we ingest it and
actually get insights from it?

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.850
So a typical month
at Quantum, we

00:05:53.850 --> 00:05:56.640
will take in over
600 million sessions.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.220
So to put that in
perspective, it's

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.320
about 300 million
unique visitors.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.310
And that's about one
ninth of the internet.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.310
That's one ninth of the
folks that are online.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:09.390
We also take 89
billion HTTPS requests.

00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:11.730
And that's, to put in
perspective of scale,

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.440
about one fifth the
number of queries

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.540
that Google search
handles in a given month.

00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.480
So-- massive amounts of scale.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.630
We handle 1 and 1/2
petabytes of network traffic,

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.600
a half of a petabyte
of cloud storage,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.120
and over 14 petabytes
of BigQuery analysis.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.440
So this is just an
overwhelming amount of data.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.010
It kind of feels like just
an onslaught of information.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:38.010
And you know, what
was fun about cloud

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:40.380
is, how do we build an
infrastructure, do that?

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.280
We don't need to.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.250
We can hit a couple buttons
in Google Console and scale.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:47.730
And we have some incredible
retailers I mentioned earlier.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:48.870
Imagine at Black Friday--

00:06:48.870 --> 00:06:50.537
I know [INAUDIBLE]
yesterday was talking

00:06:50.537 --> 00:06:53.592
about Kohl's and his
experience in cloud

00:06:53.592 --> 00:06:55.050
and being able to
scale so quickly.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:56.508
I mean, imagine a
day that you need

00:06:56.508 --> 00:06:59.190
to handle three to five
times your normal workload,

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.900
and you just hit a switch.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:04.818
It was pretty exciting on
how we can engage in cloud

00:07:04.818 --> 00:07:06.610
and have so much success
with so much ease.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:07.000
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.250
Yeah, and this can't
be an easy challenge.

00:07:09.250 --> 00:07:12.960
How are you able to support
this amount of data traffic--

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:14.030
over a petabyte?

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:15.560
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so it's--

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:16.793
they have this massive scale.

00:07:16.793 --> 00:07:18.210
How many of you
have been involved

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.820
with big data projects?

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.960
And how many of you have had
incredible insights coming out

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:27.270
of those big data projects?

00:07:27.270 --> 00:07:31.158
So for the viewer audience,
not that many, right?

00:07:31.158 --> 00:07:32.700
Everyone kind of
put their hand down.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.863
And so we collect
incredible amounts of data.

00:07:35.863 --> 00:07:37.530
But no one cares about
what you collect.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.988
I don't care about hoarding
information or hoarding things.

00:07:39.988 --> 00:07:43.120
I care about, what insights
can I deliver from that data?

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.390
And so that's where that
massive scale analysis really

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:46.720
comes into play.

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:51.690
And so when we talk
about us as a company

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:55.020
and how to get great
insights from it,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.267
I like to relate it
to flying an airline.

00:07:57.267 --> 00:07:59.350
I know all of us-- and
many of us have flown here.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.110
But many of us fly often.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:03.660
And when I'm flying to Iceland
today, and I'll tell you,

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.750
I didn't buy the jet to
take that flight to Iceland.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:08.460
I'm buying a seat, right?

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.910
And so with our
sporadic workload--

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:15.090
so we have folks coming into
our console and our platform

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.587
and trying to look at
insights and questions

00:08:17.587 --> 00:08:18.420
they want to answer.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:20.100
Are they doing well?

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.370
Is their hybrid cloud
performing well?

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:25.950
Is their on-prem and
their cloud solutions

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:27.783
performing the same or
even better in cloud?

00:08:27.783 --> 00:08:30.242
They have questions they want
to answer, but it's sporadic.

00:08:30.242 --> 00:08:32.650
It's not constantly in our
platform asking questions.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:33.669
So it's sporadic.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:37.289
It's very similar to, I need
to take a flight somewhere.

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:40.302
I need a very small
amount of compute power--

00:08:40.302 --> 00:08:41.760
or, a large amount
of compute power

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:42.885
for a small amount of time.

00:08:42.885 --> 00:08:43.860
I don't need to take--

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:45.027
I don't need to buy the jet.

00:08:45.027 --> 00:08:46.260
I want a seat on the airline.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:48.420
So we had that same
challenge with our workload.

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:52.710
And what's really exciting is
how the cloud can empower this.

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.250
And we realized quickly that
our success was really about,

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.770
how do we ingest
this data and find

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:03.100
novel ways to present insights
from that large amount of data

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:03.810
set?

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.250
And that was what was
going to differentiate us.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:06.250
It wasn't about
how we collected.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:07.830
It wasn't about how it
looked in our console.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.260
It was, what insights can we
deliver for our customers?

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:11.177
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:09:11.177 --> 00:09:14.130
Yeah, and this data scale
is handled by BigQuery,

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:14.940
as you mentioned.

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.118
And a lot of our
customers at Google Cloud

00:09:17.118 --> 00:09:19.660
are doing very innovative things
with BigQuery at the moment.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:22.230
But as many people know, it's
not the only data science

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:22.780
platform.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:23.640
You know, there
are other vendors

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.080
that offer other solutions.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.657
You mentioned a really funny
story about your son earlier.

00:09:27.657 --> 00:09:29.740
Do you mind telling the
audience about that story?

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:33.720
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so I was
writing an article for Forbes

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.290
about our experience in
cloud and different takeaways

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:39.510
that we've had at massive scale.

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:41.820
And I was writing about
BigQuery and about how

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.070
it's been transformational
for our business, how we've

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:47.640
been empowering our customers
to have new insights that they'd

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.260
never even knew
that they could ask,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.000
some automation
about the processes

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.650
that they discover
where frustration

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:53.970
is happening online.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.130
And it's hilarious because
my 9-year-old son--

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.430
his name is Cody--

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:02.073
he seriously says to me,
"But Dad, can't Amazon

00:10:02.073 --> 00:10:03.240
and Microsoft do this, too?"

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.698
And I was kind of,
like, blown away

00:10:04.698 --> 00:10:07.260
by he knew the names of
the other two large cloud

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:08.150
providers.

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:11.220
And you know, it's funny
that you ask this, Cody--

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:12.010
absolutely.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:15.420
Like, I really believe that
Amazon and Azure-- right,

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.900
the AWS and Azure-- can deliver
on massive-scale compute

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.702
and massive-scale data analysis.

00:10:20.702 --> 00:10:23.160
But the challenge that we have
is our workload is sporadic.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.577
And I don't want
to spin up, let's

00:10:24.577 --> 00:10:26.977
say, 50,000 CPUs to
do an analysis that

00:10:26.977 --> 00:10:28.560
might take a minute
or two to spin up,

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.990
run my query for five
seconds, and shut it all down.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.180
It's just not
financially feasible.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:38.610
And so, I think one of the
really unique differentiations

00:10:38.610 --> 00:10:44.550
of Google Cloud has been this
fractional lease of analysis,

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:47.775
of big compute, to chug away
at massive scale of data.

00:10:47.775 --> 00:10:49.650
And I'm going to talk
a little bit about some

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.108
of the other things
that we believe

00:10:51.108 --> 00:10:53.777
that massive compute
brings to the table

00:10:53.777 --> 00:10:56.110
when it comes to thinking
different about data analysis.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:58.230
But for us-- and what
I told my son was--

00:10:58.230 --> 00:10:59.190
absolutely.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:01.800
But fractional leasing
has been a differentiator.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:03.870
And I think if you have
either used BigQuery,

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.870
or if you know folks that are,
they'll talk to you about that.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.420
That same fractional
lease, that massive amount

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.050
of query capabilities
for a small period time,

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.690
is really one of the unique
features of BigQuery.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:17.565
STEPHANIE WONG: And the
title of this session

00:11:17.565 --> 00:11:20.053
is Apple's 1997 Promise
to Think Different.

00:11:20.053 --> 00:11:21.970
Can you talk more about
what you mean by that?

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:24.430
How are you thinking different
with your teams right now?

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:27.360
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so this
is one of the best marketing

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:28.568
campaigns of all time.

00:11:28.568 --> 00:11:30.610
And you know, I know from
looking at the audience

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.970
that there's some of us
that probably remember

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.250
this campaign very well.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:40.590
And it was talking about
Apple's engineering fortitude,

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:44.250
and likening it to geniuses
that were brave enough

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.510
to take on the
world and change it.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:47.790
It may be crazy enough--

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:49.710
I think the words
were crazy enough

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:51.265
to try to change the world.

00:11:51.265 --> 00:11:52.890
And then they actually
accomplished it.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:55.358
But it wasn't about
buying an Apple computer,

00:11:55.358 --> 00:11:57.150
and all of a sudden,
you thought different.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.480
It was simply, like, we think
different like these geniuses.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.120
What I thought was amazing--
and where I came up with this

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:04.280
concept was--

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:07.140
when we engaged with BigQuery,
we basically took our handcuffs

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:10.170
off, these handcuffs that
we had no idea existed,

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.050
in terms of massive-scale
query analysis.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.030
We can start asking
questions that we never

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:18.540
thought about asking because
now, we didn't have any rails.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:19.573
We didn't have indexes.

00:12:19.573 --> 00:12:21.990
We didn't have certain types
of queries that we could run.

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:24.812
We could run a question
across a day, a month, a year,

00:12:24.812 --> 00:12:27.270
and start getting insights that
we never really knew about.

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:31.590
So my belief is that we are now
able to think different about,

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:35.072
how do we solve business
problems for our customers,

00:12:35.072 --> 00:12:37.530
for our enterprises, in ways
that we never really explored?

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:38.447
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:12:38.447 --> 00:12:41.260
I think you also talked about
machine intelligence a bit too,

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:41.765
right?

00:12:41.765 --> 00:12:44.770
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so one of
the things that we discovered--

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:45.810
and we're a startup.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.660
We went from just a few
years ago from two people

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.820
to there are 80-plus today.

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:53.250
As that journey
happens, you start

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.950
to learn-- for me-- a little
bit about that selling process.

00:12:55.950 --> 00:12:58.003
And we used to talk.

00:12:58.003 --> 00:13:00.420
And I think it's kind of funny,
because Apple never really

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:03.660
talked about the
performance of the CPUs

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.368
and all the metrics
of memory, stuff that

00:13:05.368 --> 00:13:07.118
techies, that we think
about, and we think

00:13:07.118 --> 00:13:08.660
about we should share this.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:10.410
What I learned
was, no one really

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.865
wants this concept about
machine intelligence.

00:13:12.865 --> 00:13:14.490
We can talk about
AI, machine learning.

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.230
No one really cares about that.

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:17.730
They just want intelligence.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:19.980
They just want answers
to their challenges

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:21.510
and their business needs.

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:24.960
And so that was a journey
that we went on and started

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.020
figuring out that we can
help customers automate some

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.450
of those human processes, some
of the things that they do

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:32.280
and they spend hours or days.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.500
We can take
massive-scale compute

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.430
and solve them,
perhaps, in seconds.

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:38.640
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah,
and machine intelligence

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:39.570
here, how--

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:41.252
can you talk about
some of the examples

00:13:41.252 --> 00:13:42.960
that you've gone
through at your company?

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.085
How are you able to implement
this type of practice

00:13:45.085 --> 00:13:45.770
and thinking?

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:48.458
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, so
we've been able to take--

00:13:48.458 --> 00:13:51.000
some of the things that we've
really solved for our customers

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.010
have been, when
I go to a website

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:56.550
and I'm having a bad experience,
how do I know about it?

00:13:56.550 --> 00:13:57.870
I don't fill out surveys.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.613
I don't call the company
and tell them, hey,

00:14:00.613 --> 00:14:01.530
your website's broken.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:02.640
I just leave.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.405
So how do we figure out people
are having a bad experience?

00:14:05.405 --> 00:14:07.280
And it turns out we can
look at the behavior.

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:08.280
They're clicking reload.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.113
They're hitting the
back and forward button.

00:14:10.113 --> 00:14:12.020
They're rage clicking
the that book a flight

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:13.602
or transact button.

00:14:13.602 --> 00:14:15.060
And we can look at
those behaviors,

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:17.130
and with BigQuery, we
can start aggregating

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:20.040
around those behaviors
and discover these lead

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:21.400
to under-conversion.

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:24.570
And so we've automated
that process of discovery

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:27.750
and build that sort of human
discovery, human analysis

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:28.620
into our product.

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:30.120
So it's been really
transformational

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.490
for our enterprise
customers because they

00:14:32.490 --> 00:14:34.813
don't have to spend days
wondering is this actually

00:14:34.813 --> 00:14:35.730
impacting my business?

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.010
They can know about
it in seconds.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:40.740
Another fantastic
one was the ability

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:44.460
to understand when did a problem
actually start occurring?

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:46.600
And for our
enterprise customers,

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:48.780
they had no way to
know when the bug was

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:50.470
introduced into their system.

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:52.345
They would spend
days looking at logs

00:14:52.345 --> 00:14:54.720
and looking at release notes
and trying to figure it out.

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:56.850
What we're able to do is
spin up tens of thousands

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:00.240
of CPUs in Cloud Functions
and Google Compute

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.160
to analyze when did
this start happening

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.730
by replaying every session
that visited the website.

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.930
When did the indicator of
the issue start occurring?

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:09.570
So with massive-scale
compute, we

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:12.600
started solving problems that
our enterprise customers had

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.490
never even broached
because they just

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:17.730
didn't have 50,000,
100,000 CPUs lying around

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:18.790
in the data center.

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:20.460
So it's really been
amazing how cloud

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:21.630
has enabled us to
think different

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:23.760
about some of the challenges
that these enterprises

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:24.740
were facing.

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:28.560
And I think another one that's
been really a hot topic for us

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:32.100
in our space has been, how do we
take the high standard of care

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:33.630
with our customer data?

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.390
And a lot of it was left
to human configuration.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:38.460
We know where sensitive
information was.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:41.490
We know where there's
PCI information, or PII,

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:42.833
and either encrypt or mask it.

00:15:42.833 --> 00:15:44.250
The problem with
that is it always

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.590
left it up to human error.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:49.800
And how often was someone
looking at this type

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.210
of data and discovery?

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.365
And that discovery would happen.

00:15:53.365 --> 00:15:54.490
We needed to automate that.

00:15:54.490 --> 00:15:59.670
So we're now engaging with
Google's Sensitive Data API

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.190
and rolling this out, hopefully,
in the next short time

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:06.000
period, about instead of
waiting for humans to discover

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.250
that there's misconfiguration,
can't we just

00:16:08.250 --> 00:16:10.225
have the computer
tell us that here's

00:16:10.225 --> 00:16:12.600
an opportunity to improve the
data that we're collecting?

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:15.330
So that's just really great
ways and exciting ways

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:17.040
that we can engage
in machine learning,

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.655
massive-scale compute,
massive-scale query analysis

00:16:19.655 --> 00:16:21.030
to automate some
of the functions

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:22.660
that we had to do as humans

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.180
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, and I
think a lot of our audience

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.320
probably wants to
know how they can

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.750
implement these practices
into their own organizations

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:28.250
as well?

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:30.630
Can you talk about some
exemplary use cases for them

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:31.680
to do this?

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:35.250
MARIO CIABARRA: Yeah, I think
there's not a prescriptive way

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:39.360
that you can take this
and bring it home to all

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:41.040
of your own organizations.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:45.330
You have to take a look at, what
are some of the human processes

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:46.890
that we can automate?

00:16:46.890 --> 00:16:49.530
And as you engage in cloud
scale and think about

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.930
compute in different
ways, I do believe

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.330
it will help you
think differently

00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.340
about some of the
massive problems

00:16:56.340 --> 00:17:00.280
that you engage in at your
enterprises and startups alike.

00:17:00.280 --> 00:17:02.640
And so, here's my
thought process.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:04.025
It's a very simple process.

00:17:04.025 --> 00:17:05.400
And it just
literally the process

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:07.560
that we took to
discover, hey, how can we

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:10.530
automate some of these processes
with massive-scale compute?

00:17:10.530 --> 00:17:13.950
And it's really
about, where are there

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:17.250
tasks that we face that
consume a lot of time?

00:17:17.250 --> 00:17:20.428
How can we translate that
into a set of queries?

00:17:20.428 --> 00:17:22.470
How can we take a look at
this massive-scale data

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:26.010
and understand, here's how
we automate that process.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:28.050
Identifying key insights--
what will make people

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:31.620
engage in these results?

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:33.310
Like for us, it
was about revenue.

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:36.180
If we can talk about, if you do
this, you'll make $17 million,

00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:39.450
that's when people started
saying, OK, these are insights

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:40.500
that I want to listen to.

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:42.798
Versus hey, here's
some graphs and charts.

00:17:42.798 --> 00:17:44.340
Thank you for the
anecdotal evidence.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:46.690
I'm going to go on about my
priorities in my daily job.

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:48.210
So when we can connect
that with revenue,

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:49.627
that's where,
really, people start

00:17:49.627 --> 00:17:52.120
to engage in those results.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:55.410
And then just engaging
across teams--

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:59.020
so we learned a little bit
about cross-team, and maybe

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:02.400
the intersection
of domains, where

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.920
we believe that this is where
creativity is really born.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:07.410
And so we can get product
teams and engineering

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:10.710
and DevOps and business
teams and executive teams all

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.530
aligned on where the biggest
impacts are for their business.

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:15.512
We can start to make a--

00:18:15.512 --> 00:18:17.970
what we believe, to say the
words-- digital transformation.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:20.345
Those words mean a little bit
different for each company,

00:18:20.345 --> 00:18:22.680
but a digital transformation
on having everyone

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.440
aligned on where are my biggest
successes for my organization,

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.607
and how do we get everyone
to take action as opposed

00:18:27.607 --> 00:18:29.670
to, maybe, confirmation
bias, or everyone

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:30.820
having their own agendas?

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:32.112
So that's been pretty exciting.

00:18:32.112 --> 00:18:34.410
So following that
recipe to success,

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:36.210
we've had about three
or four iterations

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.280
on how do we think
different about our data.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:40.140
And I think it's something that
the audience could take away.

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:40.560
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank you.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:42.067
Yeah, thank you so
much for sharing

00:18:42.067 --> 00:18:44.400
some of those wins that you've
gotten with Google Cloud,

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:45.990
and kind of enabling
our audience

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:48.360
to be able to achieve their
own digital transformation.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:48.450
MARIO CIABARRA: Awesome.

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:50.470
Well Stephanie, it was
a pleasure being here.

00:18:50.470 --> 00:18:52.430
And I thank you
all for attending.

00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:54.388
STEPHANIE WONG: All right,
thank you, everyone.

00:18:54.388 --> 00:18:56.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

